<Base title="Talking Safety Signs and UK Law: The 1996 Regulations" description="What the Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996 say about spoken safety signs, quoted directly with links, so you can put it in front of your own adviser.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Compliance" title="What the Regulations say about spoken safety signs." standfirst="You don't need to take our word for the legal position. This page quotes the Regulations directly, with links, so you can put it in front of your own adviser." />

  <section class="section dark" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="wrap statute statute--marked" style="max-width: 900px;">
      <p class="mono" style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-on-dark-3);">SI 1996/341 · Regulation 2 · Interpretation</p>
      <p>“safety sign” means a sign referring to a specific object, activity or situation and providing information or instruction about health or safety at work by means of a signboard, a safety colour, an illuminated sign, an acoustic signal, <mark>a verbal communication</mark> or a hand signal;</p>
      <p>“verbal communication” means <mark>a predetermined spoken message communicated by a human or artificial voice</mark>;</p>
      <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341" class="mono" style="font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-on-dark-2); text-decoration: none;" rel="noopener">legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341 ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="h2">1. A spoken message is a safety sign</h2>
      <p>The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996 (SI 1996/341), regulation 2, define a safety sign to include one that gives information or instruction “by means of a signboard, a safety colour, an illuminated sign, an acoustic signal, a verbal communication or a hand signal”. They define verbal communication as “a predetermined spoken message communicated by a human or artificial voice”.</p>
      <p>An AudioSign is a signboard and a verbal communication on one unit. Both have been recognised forms of safety sign since 1 April 1996.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">2. When spoken signs are to be used</h2>
      <p>Schedule 1, Part I, paragraph 2.2.1: illuminated signs, acoustic signals “and/or verbal communication must be used where the occasion requires” to signal danger, to call persons to take a specific course of action, and for emergency evacuation. Paragraph 3.2 permits illuminated signs and verbal communication to be used together.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">3. Signs are a control measure</h2>
      <p>Regulation 4(1) ties signs to the risk assessment required by the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999: signs are provided where other measures cannot adequately reduce the risk. In other words, a sign on your site is there because your risk assessment relies on it. A sign that is habitually ignored is a control that is not controlling anything.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">4. What the Regulations say about effectiveness</h2>
      <p>Schedule 1 Part I, paragraph 5: the effectiveness of a sign must not be adversely affected by too many signs too close together, by poor positioning or by poor state of repair. Paragraph 11: where sight or hearing is impaired, including by PPE, measures must be taken to supplement or replace the signs concerned. Part VIII: spoken messages must be as short, simple and clear as possible, and the people they are aimed at must understand the language.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">5. The limits, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>Paragraph 5.1.5: an acoustic signal is not to be used where there is too much ambient noise. We will tell you if a location is too loud for an AudioSign to work, and we won't sell you one for it.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">6. Reasonably practicable</h2>
      <p>The duty under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is to reduce risk so far as is reasonably practicable. The courts (<em>Edwards v National Coal Board</em>, 1949) weigh the risk against the cost in money, time and trouble of reducing it, and excuse the duty-holder only where the cost is grossly disproportionate. A battery-powered sign that clips to a fence is not a grossly disproportionate cost.</p>

      <h2 class="h2">7. Enforcement</h2>
      <p>In 2024/25 HSE brought 246 prosecutions with a 96% conviction rate and fines of over £33 million, and issued over 4,400 enforcement notices. Under the Sentencing Council guideline, fines are set by turnover and culpability; a very-high-culpability breach starts at £4 million for an organisation turning over more than £50 million.</p>

      <p class="small">This page summarises the legislation as published on legislation.gov.uk and HSE guidance. It is not legal advice and does not state that any product makes a site compliant. Check the position for your site with a competent adviser.</p>

